# Do NOT add `apps/public/** merge=ours` here. It was tried, and it silently did
# the opposite of what it looks like.
#
# During a REBASE git replays our commits on top of upstream, so the merge
# machinery's "ours" is the branch being replayed ONTO (upstream/main) and
# "theirs" is our own commit. A `merge=ours` driver therefore keeps UPSTREAM's
# file and discards ours. Worse, the resulting commit becomes an empty no-op, so
# git drops it and reports the rebase as clean. Verified in a scratch repo: an
# overlay commit touching a `merge=ours` path was dropped with "patch contents
# already upstream", leaving upstream's content in place.
#
# Our apps/public shares 15 paths with upstream's marketing site, including
# next.config.mjs, package.json and public/op1.js, so this was a live risk of
# silently reverting our own config and tracker to OpenPanel's.
#
# We now rebase manually and resolve apps/public conflicts by hand, always
# keeping our version. See docs/upstream-sync.md.
